1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,412
In this short video I'll build the swimlane
template we use for every current-state map in

2
00:00:04,412 --> 00:00:05,039
this course.

3
00:00:05,039 --> 00:00:11,842
Open draw.io in your browser at app dot diagrams
dot net, and start with a blank diagram.

4
00:00:12,467 --> 00:00:13,744
First, the lanes.

5
00:00:13,744 --> 00:00:18,562
In the search box on the left, type pool,
and drag the horizontal pool onto the page.

6
00:00:18,562 --> 00:00:24,553
A pool is a container, and each lane inside
it belongs to one actor: a person, a team, or

7
00:00:24,553 --> 00:00:25,238
a system.

8
00:00:26,700 --> 00:00:28,534
Most of our processes need five lanes.

9
00:00:28,534 --> 00:00:33,457
Select the last lane and press Command D,
or Control D on Windows, to duplicate it.

10
00:00:33,457 --> 00:00:34,734
Do it twice.

11
00:00:35,633 --> 00:00:38,025
Now make the pool wide
enough for a whole process.

12
00:00:38,025 --> 00:00:42,135
Select the pool by its label on the left,
and drag the handle on its right edge.

13
00:00:43,033 --> 00:00:44,090
Rename the lanes.

14
00:00:44,090 --> 00:00:46,005
Double-click a lane label and type.

15
00:00:46,005 --> 00:00:50,568
People first: the person who starts the
process, then everyone else who touches it.

16
00:00:50,568 --> 00:00:54,643
Then the systems, here the HR
portal and a shared team sheet.

17
00:00:54,643 --> 00:00:59,055
Systems get lanes too, because
typing into a system is a hand-off.

18
00:01:01,100 --> 00:01:05,280
Below the pool, add a small legend,
so everyone draws the same way.

19
00:01:05,280 --> 00:01:10,574
A rounded box for the trigger, coloured
orange in the Style panel so it stands out.

20
00:01:12,033 --> 00:01:13,496
A plain box for a step.

21
00:01:13,496 --> 00:01:16,538
Always a verb and an object, with
the touch time written inside.

22
00:01:17,867 --> 00:01:21,025
And a diamond for a decision:
one yes or no question.

23
00:01:22,133 --> 00:01:27,462
To connect two shapes, hover over the first one
and drag one of its blue arrows onto the next.

24
00:01:27,462 --> 00:01:30,713
Then just type, and the
text goes on the arrow.

25
00:01:30,713 --> 00:01:32,396
That is where the wait time lives.

26
00:01:33,600 --> 00:01:35,632
Finally, the seven pain-point tags.

27
00:01:35,632 --> 00:01:40,659
I make one small label, colour it, then duplicate
it with Command D for the others: REKEY,

28
00:01:40,659 --> 00:01:43,259
WAIT, READ, DRAFT, CHASE,
RUBBER, and JUDGE.

29
00:02:04,033 --> 00:02:08,712
Add a title line at the top for the process,
the trigger, the end state and the number of

30
00:02:08,712 --> 00:02:09,362
hand-offs.

31
00:02:09,362 --> 00:02:13,379
Then save it with File, Save as,
and keep the dot drawio file.

32
00:02:13,379 --> 00:02:14,517
That's your template.

33
00:02:14,517 --> 00:02:18,011
Make a copy of it for every
process you map, and you're ready.

